    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51806.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 141,
    "turn_text": "Okay, on the death penalty. Well, we've had Many hearings, the chairman that we do have the votes in the committee to get it out. I believe we have the votes on the floor to get it out. The question is, will the 14th floor allow us to do it? In the Senate, we have the same scenario. And so we are just looking for the release so that we can get it passed. It is the right time when you look at American's thought process. And by the way, 20 years ago, if you pull up my floor speech on keeping the death penalty, it was pretty vicious. And I've changed my mind. I've done a 180 on it. Maybe maturity, life, meeting people, working to get Tyra Patterson released now. She wasn't on death penalty, but she was clearly wrongfully convicted. but meeting somebody get to knowing her. She is a dear friend of mine and knowing how the system can put people in that should never be there to begin with. And then to think that we can kill them before they've had a chance to prove their innocence or in a case like Tony Aponovich, where his opportunity to get out of prison was flawed because he didn't personally ask for the DNA to be tested, but his lawyer did.",
